# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# .dockerignore — D2C Platform
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# PURPOSE:
#   Controls what gets sent to the Docker build daemon as the "build context".
#   When you run `docker build .` Docker tars everything in the current directory
#   and uploads it to the daemon BEFORE the first Dockerfile instruction runs.
#
# WHY THIS MATTERS:
#   Without .dockerignore, `docker build` uploads your entire project including:
#     - .git/          (~50-200MB for a mature repo)
#     - .venv/         (~300MB of installed packages)
#     - node_modules/  (if any frontend exists)
#     - *.log files    (potentially containing secrets in stack traces)
#
#   A bloated build context means:
#     - Slow builds: all that data is uploaded and extracted every time
#     - Fat layers: COPY . . pulls in dev files that should never be in production
#     - Security risk: .env files, credentials, logs end up IN the image
#       and are readable by anyone with docker pull / docker run access
#
# RULE OF THUMB:
#   Exclude everything, then whitelist what the app actually needs to run.
#   Our app needs: app/, migrations/, mock_servers/, scripts/, requirements.txt
#   It does NOT need: .git, tests, .venv, infra docs, editor configs, local envs
#
# HOW TO CHECK WHAT'S IN YOUR IMAGE:
#   docker build -t d2c-app .
#   docker run --rm d2c-app find /app -type f | sort
#   # Anything surprising there? Add it to this file.
#
# HOW TO CHECK CONTEXT SIZE BEFORE BUILDING:
#   tar -czh . | wc -c   # bytes sent to daemon (with .dockerignore respected)
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# ── SECRETS — most critical ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# .env files must NEVER end up inside a Docker image.
# Images are often pushed to registries (GCR, Docker Hub). Anyone who can
# pull the image can run `docker run --rm d2c-app cat /app/.env` and get
# your production database password and JWT signing key.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# GCP service account keys
*.json
!package.json

# ── GIT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# .git is the biggest single directory to exclude.
# It contains full commit history, all branches, and developer email addresses.
# The app process has no use for it at runtime.
# Size impact: small repos ~10MB, mature repos ~200MB+ — all wasted in the image.
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# ── PYTHON RUNTIME ARTIFACTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# __pycache__ from your local dev machine is compiled for YOUR Python version
# and architecture. Inside the container Python recompiles from .py source.
# Including them causes:
#   - Unnecessary bytes in the image
#   - Potential import errors if your local Python version differs from the image
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd
*.py[cod]

# Local virtual environment — never copy this into the image.
# The Dockerfile runs `pip install -r requirements.txt` which creates a fresh
# install inside the container. Your local .venv has paths like
# /Users/you/.venv/lib/... that point to your machine, not the container.
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/

# ── TESTS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tests are not needed at runtime. Excluding them:
#   - Reduces image size (test files + fixtures can be large)
#   - Prevents test dependencies (pytest, aiosqlite) from being importable
#     at runtime (they shouldn't be installed in the prod image either, but
#     belt-and-suspenders)
#   - Avoids accidentally running tests on container start
tests/
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
coverage.xml

# ── INFRASTRUCTURE & DEPLOYMENT DOCS ─────────────────────────────────────────
# These files are used to BUILD and DEPLOY the image, not to RUN it.
# The running container has no use for its own Dockerfile or Cloud Build config.
infra/
# Exception: keep the Dockerfile itself excluded — container doesn't need it
Dockerfile
Dockerfile.*
docker-compose.yml
docker-compose.*.yml

# ── DOCUMENTATION ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# README and docs are useful for humans, not for the Python process running in
# the container. Excluding them keeps the image lean.
README.md
*.md
docs/

# ── EDITORS & OS FILES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# ── LOGS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Any local log files — could contain sensitive runtime data
*.log
logs/

# ── TYPE CHECKING / LINTING CACHES ────────────────────────────────────────────
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytype/

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# WHAT IS EXPLICITLY INCLUDED (everything not listed above):
#
#   app/              ← the FastAPI application
#   migrations/       ← Alembic migration scripts (run on startup)
#   mock_servers/     ← mock Shopify + Meta API servers
#   scripts/          ← seed.py and other one-off scripts
#   requirements.txt  ← pip dependencies
#   alembic.ini       ← Alembic configuration
#   pyproject.toml    ← pytest + tool configuration
#
# Total approximate build context size: ~200KB (vs ~500MB+ without this file)
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════